Introduction 
The “Introduction” section of your Study Guide is a brief overview of the topic discussed within. This 
section should give the delegates an idea of what the topic is, why it is important, and what they 
should expect during the committee discussions. Keep it brief, but informative. 
It is recommended that you write the topic introduction towards the end of the Study Guide writing 
process, so that you have a better idea of the topic yourself and can determine which parts are 
important to include. 
Key Terms and Definitions 
A short list of essential terms and definitions that are relevant to the topic. Each term should be clearly 
defined, in order to ensure that delegates are able to understand the topic discussion, without having 
any  prior  knowledge  of  the  topic.  If  you  copy  a  definition  from  a  source,  you  should  put  it  in 
quotation marks and include the source as a footnote. Key terms and definitions should be formatted 
as follows: Key Term in “Heading 2”, Definition in “Normal Text”.  
Gross Domestic Product (GDP) 
“Gross Domestic Product is the total market value of the goods and services produced by a country’s 
economy during a specified period of time. It includes all final goods and services—that is, those that 
are produced by the economic agents located in that country regardless of their ownership and that 
are not resold in any form.”
1
 
Background Information 
Provides broader historical, political, and social context of your topic. This section should explain 
how the topic came to be (historical developments), and the major factors that shaped its current state. 
This is the main -and longest- section of your Study Guide.  
Subheadings & Graphics 
You can include subheadings (Heading 2 and Heading 3), as well as diagrams, charts, tables, and 
images. For each diagram and image, you need to add a caption (legend), as well as the source. Images 
 
